Cell[TextData[{
 "The diagram is two boxes on one wire: the measurement placing its outcome \
on the pointer record, then the labeled ",
 Cell["Un-measure", "InlineCode",ExpressionUUID->
  "42b8cf72-fa81-ffe6-7247-e73fff35c5cc"],
 " block removing it. Apply the whole circuit to the input and read the \
resulting state:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->846554368,ExpressionUUID->"1c8db7a0-3aa3-299f-44b2-2e0abf567be5"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"FullSimplify", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"ComplexExpand", "[", 
    RowBox[{"Normal", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"backward", "[", "psi", "]"}], "[", "\"\<StateVector\>\"", 
      "]"}], "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"\[Theta]", " ", "\[Element]", " ", "Reals"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"\[Phi]", " ", "\[Element]", " ", "Reals"}]}], "}"}]}], 
  "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[112]:=",
 CellID->1952818843,ExpressionUUID->"473593be-64fb-e664-6eca-0af80c8fcd4e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Cos", "[", 
    FractionBox["\[Theta]", "2"], "]"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
     RowBox[{"\[ImaginaryI]", " ", "\[Phi]"}]], " ", 
    RowBox[{"Sin", "[", 
     FractionBox["\[Theta]", "2"], "]"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{4.000942743892063*^9},
 CellLabel->
  "Out[112]=",ExpressionUUID->"a511eed1-1239-4d2f-b80c-3713b1a938a1"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The state returns as exactly ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     StyleBox["cos",
      FontSlant->"Plain"], "\[ThinSpace]", 
     FractionBox[
      StyleBox["\[Theta]", "TI"], "2"]}], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox[
      StyleBox["e", "TI"], 
      StyleBox[
       RowBox[{"i", "\[Phi]"}], "TI"]], 
     StyleBox["sin",
      FontSlant->"Plain"], "\[ThinSpace]", 
     FractionBox[
      StyleBox["\[Theta]", "TI"], "2"]}]}], "}"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "79a8e286-b760-8808-2f57-fbe3c21adb52"],
 ", the input recovered including its phase: the measurement is undone \
exactly. A projective measurement is irreversible only once its record is \
discarded; while the record remains a wire, the interaction is reversible, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["V", "TI"], "\[Dagger]"], 
   StyleBox["V", "TI"], "=", 
   StyleBox["I", "TI"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "766f3dde-6e6e-8ae3-1e14-b4980b18d8c9"],
 " exactly as von Neumann wrote it."
}], "Text",
 CellID->599175116,ExpressionUUID->"7acff19c-f8f8-c05b-2af4-e8d9f97292a1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A record, then, is a wire that gates can be conditioned on, that can be \
traced out, read jointly with the system, or undone. The cost is equally \
explicit: each measurement appends a pointer qudit, so a circuit with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["k", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "36bb0aa4-099e-3511-5e58-a30ea8289c7b"],
 " measurements on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6fab4322-7f5b-c123-15a9-5a8c7aef9009"],
 " qubits evolves a state of dimension ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox["2", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["n", "TI"], "+", 
    StyleBox["k", "TI"]}]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "559c0528-76f1-864e-03a3-9d720285ec94"],
 ". That growth is not overhead; it is the cost of retaining every branch, \
and it is what the teleportation identity, the digitization theorem, and the \
un-measurement above rely on. Retaining every branch is one computational \
strategy among several, which leads to the next claim: QF has three."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1234886004,ExpressionUUID->"4c3b3e9a-7f95-6e09-75d8-b7c47a203553"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Claim 4: One object model, three computational engines", "Section",
 CellID->1955603644,ExpressionUUID->"7b834648-1855-f17e-29b1-581299e1c044"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Key feature:",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " the objects of Claims 1 through 3 never specified how they are computed. \
Underneath a circuit application are three interchangeable engines: exact \
state-vector algebra (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"Method", " ", "->", " ", "\"\<Schrodinger\>\""}]], "InlineFormula",
  
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6aa15cf4-f328-f435-1a5f-55b186c2df1f"],
 "), tensor-network contraction (the default), and a compiled stabilizer \
tableau (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"Method", " ", "->", " ", "\"\<Stabilizer\>\""}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1fa3178e-7287-38be-4550-8ee807338a84"],
 "), each suited to a different regime of symbols, structure, and scale. The \
exact state-vector path is the dense baseline, a literal ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox["2", 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "33c53063-afff-c489-61fa-aa4cc98baed3"],
 " matrix-vector product available on demand; the two engines that genuinely \
change the representation, and so deserve a closer look, are the \
tensor-network default and the stabilizer tableau, taken in turn. Because all \
three share one object model, their representations then convert into one \
another exactly."
}], "Text",
 CellID->529524776,ExpressionUUID->"08d8bcb3-70a4-55b3-38e7-35fd01513f99"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Closest competitor.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " Qiskit Aer exposes the same multi-engine idea through its ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"method", "="}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "20e38226-5aed-155d-5090-1b805e987fa7"],
 " switch across nine backends with automatic selection; what QF adds beyond \
the switch is that all three representations are one symbolic object that \
interconverts exactly, plus ",
 Cell[BoxData["StabilizerFrame"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "160727ef-d1b8-0069-3e3c-bec8e04d52b3"],
 ", an exact stabilizer-rank object that falls back automatically at the \
first non-Clifford gate and carries symbolic parameters through the tableau."
}], "Text",
 CellID->186212144,ExpressionUUID->"261df1b1-b81e-57af-4e22-e8fb39ab3df5"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The default engine: a steered tensor-network contraction", "Subsection",
 CellID->284725946,ExpressionUUID->"00ba9d2f-c328-50fc-3059-1488f6dd22c6"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Applying a circuit is, by default, a tensor-network contraction, and the \
order of the pairwise contractions determines the cost: every step produces \
an intermediate tensor, the expense is set by the largest intermediate the \
order creates (in matrix-product language, the bond dimension it requires), \
and a poor order can be exponentially more expensive than a good one on the \
same network. QF provides that choice as a ",
 Cell[BoxData["Method"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "02964792-f196-8b6e-32ae-5edf8020d9b4"],
 " sub-option, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"\"\<Path\>\"", " ", "->", " ", "\"\<Greedy\>\""}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "166d4e5f-4333-9385-3e13-601976c7e1ef"],
 " or ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<Optimal\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1c99ee20-d785-d362-44a6-078de231be48"],
 ", or an explicit list computed and inspected with ",
 Cell[BoxData["GreedyContractionPath"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "60858f89-56d8-6615-10ba-26d3636d4d7f"],
 " / ",
 Cell[BoxData["OptimalContractionPath"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2e45545e-c429-efe4-567b-4e1bedddb5ce"],
 ". Take a familiar algorithm: Grover's search for the single input a Boolean \
oracle accepts among ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["N", "TI"], "=", 
   SuperscriptBox["2", "8"], "=", "256"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "24564246-a2b2-7a9b-4c6e-6423d74748e1"],
 " candidates. The named ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<GroverPhase\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "273d1382-afb1-09c0-4ec2-8aefca445b2a"],
 " circuit is one Grover iteration ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["G", "TI"], "=", 
   StyleBox["D", "TI"], "\[ThinSpace]", 
   StyleBox["O", "TI"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "39c9e52f-ed06-87fb-6ff6-fc8884b0edc1"],
 ", the diffusion ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["D", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "46be6341-4be3-06d9-6e46-7b2b6d966c23"],
 " after the phase oracle ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["O", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "352b8971-1d3f-d4c9-6328-213b94cbbe13"],
 "; stack the peak number of them, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["k", "TI"], "=", 
   StyleBox["\[LeftFloor]",
    SpanMaxSize->1,
    FontSize->1.2 Inherited], 
   FractionBox["\[Pi]", "4"], 
   SqrtBox[
    StyleBox["N", "TI"]], 
   StyleBox["\[RightFloor]",
    SpanMaxSize->1,
    FontSize->1.2 Inherited], "=", "12"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "227c7e4b-1c68-aac8-5204-70159502f812"],
 ", on the uniform superposition and contract the whole search through a \
greedily chosen path. The search register size:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->1006536905,ExpressionUUID->"6cd9b5a9-dbed-51da-14e6-2cf2dd982320"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"n", " ", "=", " ", "8"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[113]:=",
 CellID->207506748,ExpressionUUID->"4becebe3-5bfe-fac0-7993-02cd5d88c82a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The marked input, the single accepted candidate among ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SuperscriptBox["2", "8"], "=", "256"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "312d182f-c6c7-5e1e-60d2-9188f3722564"],
 ":"
}], "Text",
 CellID->1146295856,ExpressionUUID->"1449d45f-8382-90b9-3c76-ce193634e283"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"marked", " ", "=", " ", "200"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[114]:=",
 CellID->438365529,ExpressionUUID->"444f09dd-766a-dba7-6c74-a087031ca98d"],

Cell["Build the Boolean oracle that accepts only that input:", "Text",
 CellID->174000849,ExpressionUUID->"79e9b65a-67eb-1624-2f96-2804119e7d0e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"oracle", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"BooleanFunction", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"2", "^", "marked"}], ",", " ", "n"}], "]"}]}], ";"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[115]:=",
 CellID->766726627,ExpressionUUID->"394df0e2-3b3f-7a44-6f72-e9ccbeca48ae"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "One Grover iteration ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["G", "TI"], "=", 
   StyleBox["D", "TI"], "\[ThinSpace]", 
   StyleBox["O", "TI"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5d3de1d4-e864-19c9-04c2-f89189116b13"],
 ", the diffusion after the phase oracle:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->1506609767,ExpressionUUID->"1adc8861-0296-99d3-4ae3-224bb720eb39"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"step", " ", "=", " ", 
  RowBox[{"QuantumCircuitOperator", "[", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<GroverPhase\>\"", "[", "oracle", "]"}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[116]:=",
 CellID->281363281,ExpressionUUID->"47f3727e-1788-b883-6d81-7438a2220ae9"],

